<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch04_P02_Follow_the_proof</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Follow the proof with the odd number \(n = {@nn@}\).</p>
<p>(a) The proof writes \(n = 2k + 1\). What is \(k\) here?</p>
<div>\(k =\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<p>(b) The proof says that one of \(k\) and \(k + 1\) is even. Which one, here?</p>
<div>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>
<p>(c) The proof writes \(k(k + 1) = 2m\) and then \(n^2 - 1 = 8m\). What is \(m\) here?</p>
<div>\(m =\) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>
<p>(d) The proof shows that \(n^2 - 1\) is divisible by 8 for every odd number \(n\). Is it always divisible by 16? Give an example of an odd number \(n\) for which \(n^2 - 1\) is not divisible by 16.</p>
<div>\(n =\) [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</div>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Follow each line of the proof with \(n = {@nn@}\).</p>
<ul>
<li><p>(a) \(n = 2k + 1\) gives \(k = \dfrac{n - 1}{2} = {@tk@}\). Check: \(2 \times [[if test="is(tk < 0)"]]\left({@tk@}\right)[[/if]][[if test="is(tk >= 0)"]]{@tk@}[[/if]] + 1 = {@nn@}\).</p></li>
<li><p>(b) \(k = {@tk@}\) and \(k + 1 = {@tk1@}\). The even one is [[if test="is(partok = kk)"]]\(k\)[[/if]][[if test="is(partok = kk1)"]]\(k + 1\)[[/if]].</p></li>
<li><p>(c) \(k(k + 1) = [[if test="is(tk < 0)"]]\left({@tk@}\right)[[/if]][[if test="is(tk >= 0)"]]{@tk@}[[/if]] \times [[if test="is(tk1 < 0)"]]\left({@tk1@}\right)[[/if]][[if test="is(tk1 >= 0)"]]{@tk1@}[[/if]] = {@dq@} = 2 \times {@tq@}\), so \(m = {@tq@}\), and \(n^2 - 1 = {@nsq@} = 8 \times {@tq@}\).</p></li>
</ul>
<p>(d) The proof gives \(n^2 - 1 = 8m\), and \(m = \dfrac{k(k + 1)}{2}\) is sometimes odd. For \(n = 3\), \(k = 1\) and \(m = 1\), so \(n^2 - 1 = 8\), which is not divisible by 16. The proof's ‘4 times an even number’ guarantees a factor 8 and no more.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
